AB  - The Internet of Things’ (IoT) explosive growth has raised the need for low-latency applications on wireless networks, posing problems including centralised bottlenecks, capacity limitations, and data transmission delays. The traditional cloud-based modes face difficulties in real-time requirements of latency-sensitive IoT applications like autonomous vehicles, smart healthcare, and industrial automation. To overcome these challenges, this research proposed a Decentralised Edge Intelligence (DEI) architecture that integrates the edge computing, the Global Attention Mechanism (GAM), and the YOLOv7 model to offer a localised data processing and precise decision-making. Using YOLOv7’s speed and precision, the proposed system leverages at edge nodes for effective object identification and real-time data processing. The design integrates the GAM to enhance feature extraction by offering a pertinent spatial and channel-based characteristic, ensuring a strong performance in constrained edge computing resources. Initially, the IoT devices send raw sensory data to adjacent edge nodes. To minimise the requirement for centralised cloud interactions, these nodes pre-process the data, use YOLOv7 with GAM-enhanced inference, and give IoT devices instant feedback. A hierarchical structure controls node collaboration and reduces resource allocation, while a blockchain-based authentication system ensures safe and effective network communication. Experimental results show that DEI framework reduces latency, improves throughput, decreases bandwidth consumption for dense IoT environments. Overall, these results highlight the effectiveness of integrating the YOLOv7 and GAM in enabling the low-latency IoT applications while improving the secure, efficient, and scalable edge intelligence solutions. © 2025, Scibulcom Ltd.. All rights reserved.
