AD  - National Institute of Technology, Department of Computer Applications, Kurukshetra, 136119, India
AB  - Artificial Intelligence (AI)-integrated Digital Twins (DTs) are advancing Internet of Things (IoT)-enabled Structural Health Monitoring (SHM) through intelligent sensing, cyber–physical integration, real-time assessment, and predictive infrastructure management. However, the research landscape, thematic evolution, and emerging IoT-driven technologies in AI-enabled DTs for SHM remain insufficiently characterized. A scientometric analysis of 2,930 Scopus-indexed publications, comprising English-language journal articles published between 2018 and 2026 in Computer Science, is presented in the current study. BERTopic, publication and citation analysis, Keyword Cooccurrence Networks (KCN), Country Collaboration Networks (CCN), and Document Co-citation Analysis (DCA) are integrated to examine intellectual structures, collaboration patterns, and technological evolution. Four major research domains are identified: DT Architectures and Intelligent Integration (DTAII), Smart Structural Monitoring and Sensing Systems (SMSS), AI-Enabled Structural Intelligence and Damage Analytics (ASIDA), and Predictive Intelligence and Asset Management (PIAM). Research growth accelerates after 2021, with SMSS emerging as the most mature domain and ASIDA showing rapid expansion driven by deep learning, edge intelligence, and IoT-enabled analytics. Emerging themes include Explainable AI, Physics-Informed Neural Networks, Edge AI, blockchain-enabled DTs, federated DTs, and autonomous predictive maintenance. The findings highlight key research challenges and future directions toward scalable, interoperable, secure, and intelligent IoT-enabled DT frameworks for next-generation SHM. © 2014 IEEE.
