AB  - AbstractInternet of Things (IoT) enabled earthquake resilience has advanced through real time sensing, Earthquake Early Warning (EEW), and adaptive structural control using devices such as active mass dampers and semi active damping systems. Yet, decision grade integration with code relevant performance assessment under uncertainty remains limited. This study develops a unified, uncertainty-consistent framework for evaluating and improving the earthquake resilience of IoT-enabled buildings in terms of code relevant performance. Prior work is often fragmented, frequently treats ordinal resilience and compliance indicators as continuous, and commonly applies simplified treatments of sensing and warning uncertainty that weaken performance-based decision making. A Bayesian Ordinal Structural Equation Modeling (BOSEM) framework is proposed that jointly integrates real time IoT-based structural assessment with survey and inspection-based latent variable resilience modeling, linking observed streaming signals and ordered categorical indicators within a single probabilistic system. The approach combines an ordinal measurement layer based on threshold likelihoods for survey and inspection indicators with observed streaming inputs from IoT and early warning systems, including noise, missingness, latency, packet loss, lead time, predicted intensity, uncertainty bounds, and contextual building attributes. Outputs include a code relevant resilience outcome and decision relevant posterior quantities, enabling end to end uncertainty propagation from sensing and warning through latent mechanisms to compliance relevant outcomes. The framework is demonstrated on 162 cases. Measurement results indicate strong ordinal quality, with composite reliabilities ranging from 0.83 to 0.92 and average variance extracted ranging from 0.51 to 0.65. Discriminant validity is supported by Heterotrait Monotrait ratio of correlations (HTMT) posterior means from 0.53 to 0.63, with upper credible bounds not exceeding 0.72. Posterior predictive adequacy is supported by Posterior Predictive Check (PPC) tail area probability 0.46, polychoric residual Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) 0.07, maximum absolute item level PPC residual 0.12, and share of indicators with residual exceeding 0.10 equal to 0.14. Structural inference identifies RTAQ as the strongest direct driver of Seismic Resilience Performance (SRP) with an effect of 0.38, followed by Adaptive Control Effectiveness (ACE) 0.31, with EWR 0.21 and Code Structural Integrity (CSI) 0.19, and Pr (beta > 0) from 0.971 to 0.999. Model comparison favors the full specification, including response style and cross loadings, achieving Pareto Smoothed Importance Sampling Leave One Out Information Criterion (PSIS LOOIC) 4121.6, Widely Applicable Information Criterion (WAIC) 4124.0, and a stacking weight of 0.75, while endogeneity diagnostics indicate near zero residual correlations and instrumental relevance, with conditional R squared values of 0.16 for Real Time Assessment Quality (RTAQ) and 0.12 for CSI. © 2026 Published by Elsevier Ltd on behalf of Institution of Structural Engineers.
